alled a  chemical cocktail that was making our  Rivers very dirty indeed and that is  something that at the time Rebecca  Powell who you just heard from the  minister said that she welcomed she said  it highlighted the work that the  government is doing to try to fix that  but there is still pressure this is  still something that's coming up on the  doorstep and pressure too on on the fact  that legally binding targets that were  due to be set there's been a delay to  that happening and that is something  that Therese coffee the environment  secretary said is disappointing here's  what Rebecca Powell had to say about  that delay we shouldn't have been those  targets but we're working across the  board at PACE to get them out but what  we do need to make sure is that the  right target so a huge amount of work  has gone on behind the scenes with  consultation with stakeholder workshops  working with all the parties that will  have to actually carry out the reaching  these targets so we need to make sure  they're right we're virtually there and  they're going to be announced very very  soon and when it comes to fines for  pollution incidents that we've been  hearing about I think there's no doubt  that that will be a significant amount  of money potentially the environment  agency since 2015 have retrieved a total  of 141 million pounds worth of fines so  that money now not going to the treasury  but instead going to projects to help  clean up our Waters is going to be a  positive thing but that doesn't mean  that pressure's going to weigh this  going away this is something that's come  up again and again it came up when I was  in Chester a couple of days ago ahead of  the by-election there tomorrow the lib  Dems say that this is a pathetic attempt  to save but I think what we're seeing is  the government trying to squarely put  the blame at the doorstep of water  companies but they know it's something  they need to get to grips with okay Ali  thank you  while also making climate news today  more than 30 000 Acres of Woodland were  damaged across Britain by storms last  winter that's according to data  collected by the forestry commission it  says that more than 90 percent of trees  which fall are replanted but it is  calling for landowners and Forestry  managers to plant more diverse and  resilient tree species that can better  withstand the extreme weather brought  about by climate change along with new  pests and diseases  just stop oil protesters have issued a  call for the England Captain Harry Kane  to wear an armband carrying their  message at the World Cup the group has  also threatened to escalate its campaign  of direct action previously they've  thrown soup and paint at famous works of  art which are protected by glass glass  screens but now they say they'll  consider slashing masterpieces in an  echo of action taken by the suffragettes  German companies have signed a 15-year  deal to buy liquid gas from Qatar as the  country looks to wean itself off its  dependence on Russian gas but the  arrangement raises questions over  Germany's commitment to a carbon neutral  energy Supply it also comes despite high  profile protests against qatar's human  rights record by the German football  team and ministers during the World Cup  now the UK has started to draw gas from  a recently reopened huge gas storage  facility under the North Sea as cold  winter weather sees an increase in  demand for heating but the chief  executive of British gas owner centrica  has told Sky News that what is really  needed is more government help to  convert the storage from natural gas to  hydrogen creating the world's largest  green storage facility our business  correspondent Paul Kelso has been  speaking to Chris O'Shea at the  easington gas terminal in Yorkshire  so today while we're standing in the  easington gas terminal this terminal can  process 20 of the UK's gas demands but  the brilliant thing is if you go out  this way 20 miles out to sea is the  rough storage facility that's the UK's  largest storage facility it's stopped  operating its storage in 2017 and we  reached after the earlier this year  today's Monumental because this is the  first day that we've been withdrawing  gas from the facility to put into the  system so as you can see there's not  much wind today it's quite cold and so  there's a higher demand for gas and  roughs working exactly as it should you  were asked to open this up by government  was it a mistake with hindsight to have  shut it down so in 2017 we found some  issues with some of the wells offshore  some safety issues and we looked at the  cost of refurbishing the entire facility  and the cost of that was prohibitive  based on what the market said at the  time and so we're operating this reduced  capacity what would really like to do is  to get the right regulatory framework  with government so that we can bring  this fantastic world-class facility back  to fuel capacity not only to store  natural gas but also to store hydrogen  hydrogen is the key to the UK's  decarbonization but in order to have  people adopt or have people start to use  hydrogen you've got to have a place to  store it rough could become the world's  largest hydrogen storage facility  well let's speak to Paul he joins us now  and Paul how feasible would it be to  change the storage facility Well you can  do anything with money but the first  hurdle that you have to get over is uh  Chris Ocho referred to there is the  financing of any kind of development or  expansion of the rough storage field to  be clear it's a storage facility what it  means it was a gas field it's Sandstone  buried three kilometers down underneath  uh Salt Rock so it's sealed It's a  Wonderful  um feat of geology entirely accidentally  that produced lots of methane gas which  has long been burned and now can store  methane gas has pumped back into it it's  about 20 percent of its capacity to get  it up to 100 of capacity for natural gas  would take about a billion pounds to  convert it for hydrogen storage would  cost centrica estimate and these are  very rough figures about two billion  pounds you need to replace all the steel  in the wells that go down into the  ground to pump the as it will be  hydrogen in and out the steel that's  being used at the moment is of not  sufficient grade for hydrogen it's a  much smaller molecule than methane  that's the issue you'd probably have to  raise and start again with a new  terminal at easington where I was  talking to associate what eccentric I  want is a funding agreement with the  government similar to that which is  provided for building a nuclear power  station or actually is used for the  interconnectors that connect the UK to  the gas Grid in Europe whereby you would  set a cap and a floor as it's known so  there will be an upper price on the  energy that centrika is able to sell  from uh from the rough facility hydrogen  and future methane down the line and a  floor on how low the price could go so  if the price of gas drops hard to  imagine but if it did the government  reimburse uh centrica for that money and  if it went above a certain level all  that excess profit as we are seeing at  the moment would go back to the  government and that would give a  financial uh climate in which Central  could say they could invest because they  know they would get a return on the  money the government there was some  conversations about this uh they're  stalled at the moment it hasn't gone  away but it's going to require that kind  of long-term thinking and probably some  kind of taxpayer involvement  underwriting of these projects as it is  across the energy transition to get  something as ambition as as ambitious as  a huge hydrogen story storage facility  off the ground the aim those Chris Ocho  told me is to produce energy security  and an energy transition because the  situation we're in today he told me  we're seeing fossil fuels being burned  just 